[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Custom Elements: Defining Custom HTML Elements with JavaScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Custom elements are a powerful feature of HTML5 that allow you to define your own HTML tags and associate custom behavior with them using JavaScript. This enables you to create reusable components, extend existing HTML elements, and build more modular and maintainable web applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What are Custom Elements?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Custom elements are essentially HTML tags that you define yourself. They behave like standard HTML elements but can have custom properties, methods, and styling. This allows you to encapsulate complex functionality into a single, reusable tag."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining a Custom Element"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To define a custom element, you need to use the `customElements.define()` method. This method takes two arguments:"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The tag name: This is the name you want to give your custom element. It must contain a hyphen (`-`). For example, `my-element` is valid, but `myelement` is not."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The class: This is a JavaScript class that extends `HTMLElement` and defines the behavior of your custom element."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Creating a Simple Custom Element"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's create a simple custom element called `word-count` that displays the number of words in its content."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"class WordCount extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();
    this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
  }

  connectedCallback() {
    const text = this.textContent;
    const wordCount = text.trim().split(/\s+/).length;
    this.shadowRoot.innerHTML = `<p>Word Count: ${wordCount}</p>`;
  }
}

customElements.define('word-count', WordCount);"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`class WordCount extends HTMLElement { ... }`: Defines a JavaScript class named `WordCount` that inherits from `HTMLElement`. This is the foundation of our custom element."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`constructor() { super(); this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' }); }`: The constructor initializes the element. `super()` calls the constructor of the `HTMLElement` class. `this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' })` creates a shadow DOM for the element. Shadow DOM provides encapsulation, preventing styles and scripts from the main document from affecting the custom element, and vice versa."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`connectedCallback() { ... }`: This lifecycle callback is invoked when the custom element is connected to the DOM. Inside, we get the text content of the element, calculate the word count, and then render the count inside the shadow DOM."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`const text = this.textContent;`: Retrieves the text content of the custom element."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`const wordCount = text.trim().split(/\s+/).length;`: Calculates the number of words by trimming whitespace and splitting the text by spaces."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`this.shadowRoot.innerHTML = `<p>Word Count: ${wordCount}</p>`;`: Sets the HTML content of the shadow DOM to display the word count."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`customElements.define('word-count', WordCount);`: Registers the custom element with the name `word-count` and associates it with the `WordCount` class."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now you can use the `word-count` element in your HTML:"},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"<word-count>This is some text to count.</word-count>"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"This will display "Word Count: 6"."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Lifecycle Callbacks"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Custom elements have several lifecycle callbacks that are invoked at different stages of the element's lifecycle:"},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`connectedCallback()`: Invoked when the custom element is connected to the document's DOM."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`disconnectedCallback()`: Invoked when the custom element is disconnected from the document's DOM."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`attributeChangedCallback(name, oldValue, newValue)`: Invoked when one of the element's attributes is changed. You need to specify which attributes you want to observe using the `observedAttributes` getter."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`adoptedCallback()`: Invoked when the custom element is moved to a new document."},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Observing Attributes"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"To observe attribute changes, you need to define a static getter called `observedAttributes` that returns an array of attribute names to observe. Then, you can use the `attributeChangedCallback` to respond to changes in those attributes."},{"order":31,"type":"code","text":"class MyElement extends HTMLElement {
  static get observedAttributes() {
    return ['my-attribute'];
  }

  attributeChangedCallback(name, oldValue, newValue) {
    if (name === 'my-attribute') {
      console.log(`Attribute 'my-attribute' changed from ${oldValue} to ${newValue}`);
    }
  }

  constructor() {
    super();
    this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
  }

  connectedCallback() {
    this.shadowRoot.innerHTML = `<p>My Attribute: ${this.getAttribute('my-attribute')}</p>`;
  }
}

customElements.define('my-element', MyElement);"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `MyElement` observes the `my-attribute` attribute. When the attribute changes, the `attributeChangedCallback` is invoked, and a message is logged to the console. The `connectedCallback` also displays the attribute's value."},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"<my-element my-attribute="initial value"></my-element>

<script>
  const myElement = document.querySelector('my-element');
  myElement.setAttribute('my-attribute', 'new value');
</script>"},{"order":34,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Templates and Shadow DOM"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"It's common to use templates and shadow DOM to create the internal structure of your custom elements. Shadow DOM provides encapsulation, preventing styles and scripts from the main document from affecting the custom element, and vice versa. Templates allow you to define reusable HTML structures."},{"order":36,"type":"code","text":"const template = document.createElement('template');
template.innerHTML = `
  <style>
    p {
      color: blue;
    }
  </style>
  <p>This is a custom element with shadow DOM.</p>
`;

class ShadowElement extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();
    this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
    this.shadowRoot.appendChild(template.content.cloneNode(true));
  }
}

customElements.define('shadow-element', ShadowElement);"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, a template is created with a paragraph and some CSS. The `ShadowElement` then clones the template's content and appends it to its shadow DOM. This ensures that the styles defined in the template are scoped to the custom element."},{"order":38,"type":"code","text":"<shadow-element></shadow-element>"},{"order":39,"type":"subheading","text":"Extending Existing HTML Elements"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also extend existing HTML elements by specifying the `extends` option in the `customElements.define()` method. This allows you to add custom behavior to standard HTML elements."},{"order":41,"type":"code","text":"class FancyButton extends HTMLButtonElement {
  constructor() {
    super();
  }

  connectedCallback() {
    this.style.backgroundColor = 'lightblue';
    this.style.padding = '10px';
    this.style.border = 'none';
    this.style.borderRadius = '5px';
    this.style.cursor = 'pointer';
  }
}

customElements.define('fancy-button', FancyButton, { extends: 'button' });"},{"order":42,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `FancyButton` extends the `HTMLButtonElement`. The `connectedCallback` is used to style the button. To use this custom element, you need to specify the `is` attribute on a standard `<button>` element."},{"order":43,"type":"code","text":"<button is="fancy-button">Click Me</button>"},{"order":44,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":45,"type":"paragraph","text":"Custom elements are a powerful tool for creating reusable and modular web components. By understanding the lifecycle callbacks, shadow DOM, and the ability to extend existing elements, you can build complex and maintainable web applications with ease."}]
